🔹 What is @FunctionalInterface?

👉 It is an annotation in Java used to indicate that an interface is a functional interface
(i.e., it has only one abstract method)

✅ 1. Ensures Only One Abstract Method
👉 If you write more than one abstract method → compile-time error
@FunctionalInterface
interface Test {
    void method1();
    // void method2(); ❌ Error
}

✅ 2. Annotation is Optional (But Recommended)
👉 Even without annotation, interface can still be functional
interface Test {
    void method1();
}
✔ Works fine with lambda
👉 But annotation gives safety

✅ 3. Supports Lambda Expressions
👉 Functional interfaces support lambda

@FunctionalInterface
interface Square {
    int calc(int n);
}

Square s = n -> n * n;

✅ 4. Can Have Multiple Default & Static Methods
👉 Only restriction is on abstract methods
@FunctionalInterface
interface Demo {
    void show();  // only one abstract method

    default void m1() {}
    static void m2() {}
}
✔ Valid

✅ 5. Can Override Methods from Object Class
👉 These do NOT count as abstract methods
@FunctionalInterface
interface Demo {
    void show();//abstract method
    boolean equals(Object obj); // allowed bcoz it come from Object class
}

❓ Why no error?
👉 Because:
equals() is already present in Object class
So Java does NOT count it as a new abstract method
"Ignore methods that already exist in Object class"
"Methods inherited from Object class like equals(), hashCode(), toString()
are not counted while checking functional interface."

"Object class provides methods like toString() for printing, equals()
 for comparison, hashCode() for hashing, getClass() for runtime info,
 clone() for copying,
and wait/notify methods for thread communication."
